WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.568
Last time, every clinic got a line
of its own, all with the same tilt.

00:00:04.608 --> 00:00:09.786
Alder's line sits 0.87 above
Cedar's. At the lower-risk end,

00:00:09.826 --> 00:00:15.134
at the higher-risk end, anywhere
you look: the same 0.87.

00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:21.968
On the log-odds ruler, a step of
0.87 multiplies the odds by 2.4.

00:00:22.008 --> 00:00:28.097
So in some sense, Alder is 2.4
times Cedar. But 2.4 times what?

00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:30.881
Here are three ways to finish that sentence.

00:00:30.921 --> 00:00:34.721
One: Alder has 2.4 times as many readmissions.

00:00:34.761 --> 00:00:40.652
Two: at Alder, each patient is 2.4
times as likely to be readmitted.

00:00:40.692 --> 00:00:46.604
Three: at Alder, each patient's odds
of readmission are 2.4 times as high.

00:00:46.644 --> 00:00:50.300
Only one of them is right.
Pick one before we go on.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.121
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

00:00:54.161 --> 00:00:56.090
Today: reading gamma.

00:00:58.167 --> 00:01:00.303
Start with the first, and count.

00:01:00.343 --> 00:01:04.129
Alder had 13 readmissions among 30 patients.

00:01:04.169 --> 00:01:09.668
Cedar had 8 among 20. That is
43% against 40%: almost level.

00:01:09.708 --> 00:01:12.431
And we already know why
raw counts settle nothing:

00:01:12.471 --> 00:01:14.922
they depend on who each clinic treats.

00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:20.488
The second reading: each patient is
2.4 times as likely to come back.

00:01:20.528 --> 00:01:27.356
Take a lower-risk patient. The model
gives 18% at Cedar and 34% at Alder.

00:01:27.396 --> 00:01:30.216
That is 1.9 times, not 2.4.

00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:36.126
For a higher-risk patient, 62%
becomes 80%: only 1.3 times.

00:01:36.166 --> 00:01:41.934
And it could never be 2.4. That
would be a chance of nearly 150%.

00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:45.526
Now the third. Turn those
probabilities into odds.

00:01:45.566 --> 00:01:53.440
For the lower-risk patient: 0.22
at Cedar, 0.52 at Alder. 2.4 times.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:02:00.246
For the higher-risk patient:
1.64 and 3.91. 2.4 times again.

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:06.048
That is what a gap between two gammas means.
For patients who look the same on paper,

00:02:06.088 --> 00:02:11.558
one at each clinic, the odds differ by
a single multiplier: the odds ratio.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:18.192
Here are the two clinics as S-curves:
one shape, slid sideways by that gap.

00:02:18.232 --> 00:02:23.190
Now slide a patient along, from low
risk to high, and read both curves.

00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:29.224
A patient with a 5% chance at Cedar
has 11% at Alder: 6 points more.

00:02:29.264 --> 00:02:35.922
At 18%, where Cedar's lower-risk
patients sit, Alder gives 34%: 16 points.

00:02:35.962 --> 00:02:39.037
At 50%, it is 70%: 20 points.

00:02:39.077 --> 00:02:44.520
At 62%, Cedar's higher-risk
patients: 80%. That's 18 points.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:48.195
And at 90%, 96%: back down to 6.

00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:49.905
One gap, one odds ratio.

00:02:49.945 --> 00:02:55.461
But the difference in probability swells
in the middle and fades at both ends.

00:02:55.501 --> 00:03:01.128
The ratio of probabilities shrinks
all the way: 2.2 at the low end,

00:03:01.168 --> 00:03:07.621
1.06 at the top. Only when readmission
is rare does it come close to 2.4.

00:03:07.661 --> 00:03:12.598
So what a gap costs in readmissions
depends on which patients a clinic sees.

00:03:15.167 --> 00:03:19.927
Last time we said: read the gaps, not
the gammas themselves. Here is why.

00:03:19.967 --> 00:03:23.355
A gamma is read where
every risk factor is zero.

00:03:23.395 --> 00:03:26.888
So far, zero has meant a lower-risk patient.

00:03:27.033 --> 00:03:31.238
But that was our choice. Call the
higher-risk patient zero instead.

00:03:31.278 --> 00:03:35.164
Every gamma climbs by 2.02: the whole step.

00:03:35.204 --> 00:03:39.782
Or put zero halfway between,
at a patient who doesn't exist.

00:03:39.822 --> 00:03:43.788
Now every gamma is 1.01
above where it started.

00:03:43.933 --> 00:03:49.120
Through all of this, no line has moved,
and no patient's probability has changed.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:54.026
And the gap between Alder
and Cedar is 0.87 every time.

00:03:54.066 --> 00:03:57.723
A gamma depends on where
we put zero. A gap doesn't.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.287
One warning before we finish.

00:04:01.327 --> 00:04:04.911
Suppose all 103 patients went to Cedar.

00:04:04.951 --> 00:04:11.063
The model says 38% would come
back. All of them at Alder: 55%.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:18.285
Put those two rates on the log-odds
ruler. They are 0.69 apart, not 0.87.

00:04:18.325 --> 00:04:24.356
Nothing has gone wrong. The 0.87
compares one kind of patient at a time.

00:04:24.396 --> 00:04:31.000
The 0.69 compares two averages, taken
over patients of very different risk.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:32.740
Those are different questions,

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:37.363
and the second gives the smaller answer
whenever patients differ in risk.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:42.054
So if a gap from a model looks bigger
than the gap between two overall rates,

00:04:42.094 --> 00:04:44.293
that alone is no contradiction.

00:04:45.867 --> 00:04:47.363
Let's gather it up.

00:04:47.403 --> 00:04:52.760
A gap between gammas multiplies the odds,
by the same amount for every patient.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.814
What that does to a probability
depends on where the patient starts.

00:04:56.854 --> 00:05:00.510
And gammas depend on where
zero is. Gaps do not.

00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:05.079
One reminder: every gap we read
today rests on 103 patients,

00:05:05.119 --> 00:05:09.062
so each one is rough. How
rough is still to come.

00:05:09.102 --> 00:05:13.244
And all this time, the lines have
simply appeared, already in place.

00:05:13.284 --> 00:05:17.696
Next time, we find out how the dial
and the sliders settle where they do:

00:05:17.736 --> 00:05:19.244
fitting by balance.

00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:23.472
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
